Protein was the predominant insect product of interest in most of the LCA studies analysed due to the promising potential to substitute some conventional protein sources deemed to be less environmentally sustainable. The insect biomass’s protein content mainly depends on the insect diet used for rearing. Although low-quality feeding material is cheaper and often less impactful, protein quality and yield may be negatively affected. As a result, using a low-quality insect diet can lead to a potential increase in impacts since more collection, transport, and feed pre-treatment is required to obtain the same protein yield compared with using a higher quality feed [82,169]. Bosch et al. [169] reported a lower GWP score (68%) for proteins obtained from BSFL reared on residual materials than edible food products for humans. Other factors, such as the extraction methods applied to


