Regarding energy use for different waste management systems, including the BSFL biowaste treatment, Komakech et al. [165] reported that anaerobic digestion and composting consumed less energy than the BSFL compost treatment, while vermicomposting consumed more. In contrast, Mondello et al. [167] concluded that the BSFL biotreatment of food waste was more energy-efficient than landfilling, incineration, composting, and biogas scenarios. Transitioning to alternative energy sources such as renewable energy can mitigate some of the impacts related to energy consumption. Salomone et al. [70] performed a sensitivity analysis on alternative energy sources for the drying process. They found that using photovoltaic and natural gas was more efficient—45.58% and 4.65%, respectively—than the baseline scenario of electrical energy from the Italian mix. Similarly, Smetana et al. [83] found that using long-term photovoltaic energy could reduce impacts by 25%. Thus, there is a need to consider renewable energy sources for operating a BSFL facility to save on energy consumption.


